# PROPOSED progress.js CHANGE — the integration spec, as an applicable patch
#
# NOT APPLIED. tools/ is in the daemon's SKIP_DIRS; the repo root is not. A .js
# written there goes live in the running game within ~150ms, and this patch
# changes what the run spends its money on and when it prestiges. The lead
# deploys, not me.
#
#   cd ~/Repos/bitburner-scripts
#   patch -p0 --dry-run < tools/staging/augplan/progress.js.diff   # check first
#
# The full working file is tools/staging/augplan/.progress.new.js.
#
# =============================================================================
# BEFORE APPLYING — three things, in this order
#
# 1. augplan.js must exist at the REPO ROOT, because the patch imports it by the
#    bare in-game name. There is already a DIFFERENT staged module claiming that
#    name: tools/staging/augplan.js, which solves the cost half of this problem
#    and is imported by tools/staging/sing-augbuy.js and sing-augsurvey.js.
#    Deploying both under one name is a collision. See NOTES.md §2 and §6 — they
#    should be merged, and the older one has two bugs this one does not (its
#    SOA_AUGS list is missing the "SoA - " prefix so isSoa() matches nothing, and
#    it prices SoA augmentations as order-independent when queued copies do in
#    fact escalate each other 7x).
#
# 2. Re-run tools/test/ramoverride.test.mjs AND fix its subject. The RAISE_CEILING
#    note in the patch explains why: the shipped constant is already 5GB short of
#    the shipped file's real price, and R5 does not see it because it checks
#    tools/staging/ramoverride/progress.js, which is a stale copy. That is a
#    live latent failure independent of this change and should be fixed first.
#
# 3. npm run verify after pushing. CLAUDE.md: the auto-push is not reliable, and
#    two edits have already reached nothing while reporting success.
#
# =============================================================================
# WHAT THE PATCH DOES — the architecture, in three moves
#
# PASS N (every five minutes):
#   1. COMPUTE THE PLAN. Gather every offer from every joined faction with its
#      live price, rep requirement, multipliers and prerequisites, and hand the
#      lot to planPurchases() with the current cash. Buy NOTHING.
#   2. RUN THE GATE ON THE PLANNED M. installgate.js's marginal-versus-average
#      rule is unchanged; only its input moves from the queued set to
#      (already-queued set) x (planned set).
#   3. WHEN THE GATE FIRES: execute the plan in order, re-pricing each purchase
#      against the game and stopping loudly on drift, then install.
#
# WHY THE GATE HAD TO MOVE. If purchasing waits for the install, nothing is ever
# queued, so the old gate's M is permanently 1 and shouldInstall permanently
# returns "the queued augmentations give no gain". The gate would never fire
# again. Evaluating the PLANNED set is not a workaround for that — it is
# strictly better, because the gate then sees the value of what the money could
# buy, including a threshold reputation has just crossed, before a dollar is
# spent. The marginal rate still rises while purchases are still becoming
# possible and still flattens when money and reputation stop unlocking anything,
# which is the only property the rule depends on.
#
# THE NO-THRASH ARGUMENT SURVIVES, DIFFERENTLY. installgate.js's header argues
# the gate cannot fire twice in a row because an install leaves $1,262 and M is
# therefore 1. That argument now runs through the planner instead of the queue:
# nothing is affordable at $1,262, so the plan is empty and M is 1. Same
# conclusion, different mechanism — so it is asserted rather than assumed, in
# augplan.test.mjs [AP9].
#
# WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT CHANGED
#   - installgate.js itself. Its rule, its thresholds and its refusal messages
#     are untouched; only progress.js's choice of what to feed it moves.
#   - The advisory (no-SF4) path, which is what this save actually runs today.
#   - homeup.js --reserve 0 before an install (CLAUDE.md's install procedure
#     step 2). The planner spends money on augmentations; whatever it leaves
#     still has to go into home RAM, which survives the prestige. That remains
#     the caller's job and is NOT in this patch.
#
# =============================================================================
